# What `settings.json` allows, and what it leaves to ask

`settings.json` in this folder decides which commands run without an
approval. Every command `AGENTS.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md` tell the agent to
run either matches a rule in `permissions.allow` or is in a table below, left
to ask on purpose. A command missing from the allow list is a decision, not an
omission, and this file says which and why. A test in the template this
repository was rendered from holds the documents and the list against each
other, so a new command in either document changes this file or the list.

## Asks on purpose

| Command | Why it asks |
| --- | --- |
| `git worktree remove` | It deletes the worktree's ignored files, a local data file included, without a word. `AGENTS.md` asks for `git status --porcelain --ignored` in the worktree first, and a rule would run the removal before anyone read that list. |
| `uv add` | `CONTRIBUTING.md` has Hypothesis join the `dev` group the first time a property-based test is written, and adding it rewrites `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`. The approval is where the person sees the package and any index option before anything is fetched; see the `uv` rules below. |
| `uv lock` | No document tells the agent to run it on its own: `uv sync --locked` fails when the lockfile disagrees with `pyproject.toml`, and changing the lockfile is the person's call. The approval is also where an index option is seen; see the `uv` rules below. |
| `git push` | It publishes the branch to the host, where other people can read it, and a project is public by default. The approval is the person seeing what leaves the machine. |
{% if host == 'gitlab' %}| `glab mr create` | The same: it publishes a title and a description to the host. |
| `glab issue create` | The same, for an issue. |
| `glab mr merge` | It runs only on the person's word for that merge request, as `AGENTS.md` says; a rule would let a yes for one carry to the next. |
| `glab mr rebase` | It rewrites the merge request's branch on the host. |{% else %}| `gh pr create` | The same: it publishes a title and a description to the host. |
| `gh issue create` | The same, for an issue. |
| `gh release view --repo coolbress/plinth` | It reads another repository, plinth, over the network, and only before saying plinth lacks something; a rule naming a repository would pass reads of that repository with any other option too. |
| `gh pr merge` | It runs only on the person's word for that pull request, as `AGENTS.md` says; a rule would let a yes for one carry to the next. |
| `gh pr update-branch` | It pushes a merge commit to the pull request's branch on the host and starts its checks again; only the session merging the open pull requests runs it, one at a time, as `CONTRIBUTING.md` step 7 says. |{% endif %}

## Named only as what not to do

`AGENTS.md` names these as commands never to run, so no rule allows them. What
`permissions.deny` stops outright is listed in `AGENTS.md`.

| Command | Why no rule |
| --- | --- |
{% if host == 'gitlab' %}| `glab auth login` | A refusal is never answered by a wider token; the person changes a setting instead. |{% else %}| `gh auth refresh` | A refusal is never answered by more scopes; the person changes a setting instead. |
| `scripts/with-admin-token.sh` | Typed by the person, in their own terminal, with a credential the session never holds. |{% endif %}
| `bash -c` | It runs a string as a shell, where a deny rule cannot see the command; only the sandbox stops what it does. |

## What the deny rules catch

`AGENTS.md` names what `permissions.deny` stops; this section says which
spellings each rule catches and what it leaves.

- The token rules: {% if host == 'gitlab' %}`glab`'s token, from `glab auth status --show-token` or `-t` or
  `glab config get token`.{% else %}`gh`'s token, from `gh auth token` or from `gh auth status --show-token`
  or `-t`. A spelling with the options in another order, such as `-t` after
  `--hostname`, matches no deny rule and no allow rule: in a mode that asks,
  the person approves or refuses it, and in auto mode the classifier decides.{% endif %}
- The `.env` rules stop the Read tool, `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `sed`, `grep`,
  `<`, `.` and `source`, and a `cat` or `grep` of `.env` inside `$(...)`, a
  subshell or a group; not `bash -c`, another reader inside `$(...)`, a search
  that does not name the file, or a script that opens it, which only the
  sandbox stops.
- The force-push rules read the command's text. They deny `--force`,
  `--force-with-lease` and `--force-if-includes` anywhere after `git push`,
  `-f` as an argument of its own, a `+` refspec, the same with options between
  `git` and `push` (`git -C <dir>`), and each part of a command chained with
  `&&` or `;`. Any git command whose text has ` push ` with a `+`, `--force` or
  ` -f` after it is refused too, such as a commit message or a branch name;
  reword it. They do not stop a path to git (`/usr/bin/git push --force`),
  `sh -c 'git push --force'`, a git alias, combined short flags such as `-uf`,
  or `--mirror`, and in auto mode no prompt asks about those: the classifier
  decides, and it has let a force push through. The boundary is the host's:
  {% if host == 'gitlab' %}the protected branch{% else %}the ruleset{% endif %} refuses a force push to `main`, and nothing refuses one
  to another branch.
- The shell-write rules (a `cat` heredoc, `tee`, `sed -i`) keep the agent's
  edits where the person can review them. A denied call is not a prompt and
  ran nothing, not even the commands chained before it: write the file with
  the edit tools and run those commands on their own.

## Why the allowed rules have the shape they have

- `Bash(git fetch origin)` is the exact command, with no `:*`. A rule without
  `:*` matches that text and nothing longer: `git fetch origin main` and
  `git fetch` ask (measured on Claude Code 2.1.307). A `git fetch:*` prefix
  would also pass `git fetch --upload-pack=<program>`, which runs a program,
  and a refspec that rewrites local branches.
- `Bash(git worktree list:*)` and `Bash(git worktree add --no-track -b:*)`
  are prefixes below the subcommand: neither reaches `git worktree remove`.
  The `add` rule is the spelling `AGENTS.md` gives, not `git worktree add:*`:
  that prefix would also pass `git worktree add -B <branch>`, which resets an
  existing branch's tip, while `-b` refuses when the branch exists and git
  refuses `-b` together with `-B` or `--detach`. So the allowed form writes
  only a new branch and a folder in the checkout: the folder goes with
  `git worktree remove`, the branch with `git branch -D` after it, and
  nothing else changes. It is the first step of every task.
- `Bash(uv sync --locked)` and `Bash(uv build)` are the exact spellings the
  documents and CI use, with no `:*`. A `uv sync:*`, `uv lock:*` or
  `uv build:*` prefix also passed an index option naming any host:
  `uv sync --index-url <url>` ran with no prompt and sent its requests to
  that host. An index option (`--index-url`, `--default-index`, `--index`,
  `--extra-index-url`, `--find-links`) sends a request to a host chosen by
  whatever text the agent read, with whatever the URL carries, so every one
  asks: the person sees the host before anything is fetched. The same holds
  for an environment assignment in front,
  `UV_INDEX_URL=<url> uv sync --locked` (measured on Claude Code 2.1.310).
  `uv lock` has no rule because no document tells the agent to run it on its own.
